"[2602.13530v1] REMem: Reasoning with Episodic Memory in Language Agent (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13530v1)\nciteturn31888view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13530v1\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Feb 2026 (this version), latest version 28 Feb 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:REMem: Reasoning with Episodic Memory in Language Agent\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yiheng Shu , cite7†Saisri Padmaja Jonnalagedda , cite8†Xiang Gao , cite9†Bernal Jiménez Gutiérrez , cite10†Weijian Qi , cite11†Kamalika Das , cite12†Huan Sun , cite13†Yu Su L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled REMem: Reasoning with Episodic Memory in Language Agent, by Yiheng Shu and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Humans excel at remembering concrete experiences along spatiotemporal contexts and performing reasoning across those events, i.e., the capacity for episodic memory. In contrast, memory in language agents remains mainly semantic, and current agents are not yet capable of effectively recollecting and reasoning over interaction histories.\nL17: We identify and formalize the core challenges of episodic recollection and reasoning from this gap, and observe that existing work often overlooks episodicity, lacks explicit event modeling, or overemphasizes simple retrieval rather than complex reasoning. We present REMem, a two-phase framework for constructing and reasoning with episodic memory: 1) Offline indexing, where REMem converts experiences into a hybrid memory graph that flexibly links time-aware gists and facts.\nL18: 2) Online inference, where REMem employs an agentic retriever with carefully curated tools for iterative retrieval over the memory graph. Comprehensive evaluation across four episodic memory benchmarks shows that REMem substantially outperforms state-of-the-art memory systems such as Mem0 and HippoRAG 2, showing 3.4% and 13.4% absolute improvements on episodic recollection and reasoning tasks, respectively. Moreover, REMem also demonstrates more robust refusal behavior for unanswerable questions.\nL19: Comments:  | Accepted by The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2026) as poster\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2602.13530 [cs.AI]\nL22:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2602.13530v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL23:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.13530†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Yiheng Shu [cite19†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Fri, 13 Feb 2026 23:54:55 UTC (4,816 KB)\nL28: cite20†[v2] Fri, 20 Feb 2026 21:41:26 UTC (4,817 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v3] Sat, 28 Feb 2026 19:29:37 UTC (4,816 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled REMem: Reasoning with Episodic Memory in Language Agent, by Yiheng Shu and 7 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite14†View PDF L38:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite21†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite22†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.AI\nL45: \nL46: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L47: \nL48: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2026-02 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite28†cs L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX 